 624		\leanok
 625		\uses{def:ld-question-distribution, def:ld-combined-strategy}
 626		Sample a question pair of the directly indexed game with parameters
 627		$\mathsf{ldparams}^{\mathrm c}$, and let $\mathbf{i}$ be the coordinate index
 628		of its common sample, uniform on $\{0,1,\ldots,m+k-1\}$.  Conditioned on
 629		$\mathbf{i} < m$, an event of probability $m/(m+k)$, the pair of questions
 630		measured by $\mathcal S^{\mathrm c}$ in Definition~\ref{def:ld-combined-strategy}
 631		is distributed as the question pair of the directly indexed game with
 632		parameters $\mathsf{ldparams}$ conditioned on the same ordered type pair.
 633		Conditioned on $\mathbf{i} \ge m$, both measured questions are the point
 634		question at the point part of the sampled point, and that point part is
 635		uniform on $\F_q^m$; that is, the measured pair is a point-versus-point pair
 636		of the game with parameters $\mathsf{ldparams}$.
 637	\end{lemma}
 638	\begin{proof}
 639		\leanok
 640		\uses{def:ld-question-distribution, def:ld-combining-map}
 641		The common sample of the combined game consists of a uniform point
 642		$(u,\alpha) \in \F_q^{m+k}$, a uniform index $\mathbf{i}$, and a uniform
 643		direction $(v,w)$, the three independent.  Conditioned on $\mathbf{i} < m$,
 644		the triple $(u, \mathbf{i}, v)$ is uniform on
 645		$\F_q^m \times \{0,\ldots,m-1\} \times \F_q^m$, which is the common sample of
 646		the game with parameters $\mathsf{ldparams}$, and $\alpha$ and $w$ are
 647		independent of it.  Under this conditioning the coordinate direction of an
 648		axis-parallel question and the prefix projection of a diagonal direction have
 649		point parts equal to the coordinate direction and the prefix projection
 650		computed in $\F_q^m$, by the coordinate order fixed in
 651		Definition~\ref{def:ld-combining-map}, and cases~2 and~4 of
 652		Definition~\ref{def:ld-combined-strategy} measure exactly the canonical
 653		question of the game with parameters $\mathsf{ldparams}$ attached to that
 654		sample.  Conditioned on $\mathbf{i} \ge m$, every direction produced by the
 655		question distribution has vanishing point part, so cases~1, 3 and~5 apply and
 656		all measure the point question at $u$, which is uniform.
 657	
 658		The two conditionings are legitimate because the coordinate index and
 659		the two point parts of the common sample are jointly uniform on
 660		$\{0,\ldots,m+k-1\} \times \F_q^m \times \F_q^m$, so the index is
 661		independent of the point parts.
 662	\end{proof}
