 791	\begin{proof}
 792		\leanok
 793		\uses{lem:ld-combining-collision, lem:ld-combining-exact-linearity,
 794			lem:ld-combining-split, def:bracket, lem:schwartz-zippel-total-degree}
 795		The first conclusion for $\mathcal S^{\mathrm c}$ says that, on average over
 796		a uniform $(u,\alpha) \in \F_q^{m+k}$, the value $p(u,\alpha)$ of the outcome
 797		$p$ of $G^{\mathrm B}$ agrees with the combined point answer
 798		$\sum_r \alpha_r b_r$ computed from the outcome $b$ of the point measurement
 799		$\widehat A^{\mathsf{Point},u}$ of $\mathcal S$ at $u$, up to $\delta$.
 800	
 801		The outcome is a combined polynomial with probability at least
 802		$1 - O(\delta + (m+k)d/q)$.  Indeed, condition on an outcome $p$ that is not
 803		combined.  By Lemma~\ref{lem:ld-combining-exact-linearity}, $p(u,\cdot)$ fails
 804		to be a linear form except with probability $md/q$ over $u$; the combined
 805		point answer is a linear form in $\alpha$ by construction; and two distinct
 806		polynomials in $\alpha$ of total degree at most $kd$ agree at a uniform
 807		$\alpha$ with probability at most $kd/q$ by
 808		Lemma~\ref{lem:schwartz-zippel-total-degree}.  Hence, conditioned on a
 809		non-combined outcome, the agreement above holds with probability at most
 810		$(m+k)d/q$, and comparing with the bound $\delta$ gives the claim.
 811	
 812		Replacing $G^w$ by $H^w$ therefore changes each of the first two relations by
 813		at most $O(\delta + (m+k)d/q)$, since the two measurements differ only on the
 814		event that the outcome is not combined, and
 815		$\mathrm{split}(\mathrm{comb}_g) = g$ on its complement; the third relation
 816		for $H^{\mathrm A}$, $H^{\mathrm B}$ follows from the third relation for
 817		$G^{\mathrm A}$, $G^{\mathrm B}$ by data processing, $H^w$ being a
 818		post-processing of $G^w$.
 819	
 820		It remains to upgrade the first relation from the combined value to the tuple
 821		of values.  Let $g$ be the outcome of $H^{\mathrm B}$ and $b$ the outcome of
 822		the relabeled point measurement $\widehat A^{\mathsf{Point},u}$.
 823		If $(g_0(u),\ldots,g_{k-1}(u)) \neq b$, then by
 824		Lemma~\ref{lem:ld-combining-collision} the combined values
 825		$\sum_r \alpha_r g_r(u)$ and $\sum_r \alpha_r b_r$ agree with probability at
 826		most $1/q$ over a uniform $\alpha$.  Averaging over $\alpha$ therefore
 827		converts the relation for the combined values into the tuple relation
 828		\[
 829			\widehat A^{\mathsf{Point},\,u}_{a_0,\ldots,a_{k-1}} \ot \Id_{\mathrm B}
 830			\simeq \Id_{\mathrm A} \ot
 831				H^{\mathrm B}_{[(g_0(u),\ldots,g_{k-1}(u)) =
 832					(a_0,\ldots,a_{k-1})]}
 833		\]
 834		with error $O(\delta + (m+k)d/q)$, and symmetrically for the second relation.
 835	\end{proof}
